Local-MP lockstep: event-driven waits, a client-pacing hook, and the profilers
that found the connected-play frame cost.

The two linked consoles run melonDS's MP lockstep over the loopback bridge: the
host blocks in RecvReplies for each client's reply to its CMD frame, and a client
blocks in RecvHostPacket for the host's next frame (its NextSync gate). Both
waits polled with sleep_for(1ms), so every hand-off - several a frame, on both
sides - cost a sleep tick, more under load. WaitReadable() select()s the socket
with the same deadline instead, and returns the moment the datagram lands.

nds_wifi_is_mp_client() (and Wifi::IsMultiplayerClient) tells a frontend that
this console is an associated MP client whose emulated time the host already
paces, so it can skip its own audio-clock throttle - a client asleep in that
throttle when the host's CMD lands made the host wait out the sleep, and on one
PC that phase-locked the pair into taking turns (~24 fps each).

RecvHostPacket now accepts only the parent's frames once the parent is known
(host_only): with a second child present, SendGeneric broadcasts that child's
frames too, and handing them to a client as host data corrupted its MP stream
This was a direct edit to the framework and is captured here.

Removed: the unconditional "[local_mp] DIAG ... 3rd sender" line in
DrainSocket, a probe that logged every frame from a third instance -
several lines a frame in a 3-4 player session.

Diagnostics, all OFF unless their env var is set:
  LSW2_MP_PROF=1   once a second, per blocking wait: calls, total blocked ms,
                   max, timeouts; and (Wifi.cpp MPSyncProf) the client's
                   runahead after each ACK vs where the next CMD landed.
  LSW2_MP_TRACE=1  one line per bridge event on a steady-clock microsecond base
                   shared by all instances, so two consoles' logs merge.

